From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pear <pear@localhost>
Date: Mon, 17 Sep 2001 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [storage] feat: the recurrent-state pages a later lookup no
 longer loads are demoted

Local, from no pull request.

For a hybrid (recurrent-state) model LMCache stores one recurrent-state page per chunk per object group, but a later
lookup on the same prefix loads only the last `window` chunks of such a group: the earlier pages are dead weight.
end_session touches every key of a session, so the dead pages stay as recent as live data and an eviction round
takes live prefixes instead. An on_l1_keys_demoted hook goes through the listener, the L1 managers, StorageManager and
the eviction policy (the LRU moves demoted keys to its least-recently-used end and never promotes them again); the
pages outside the window are demoted where the prefetch controller releases their read locks; end_session touches
only the usable keys (usable_key_indices), the window's pages first, so that the LRU's reversed walk leaves them most
recent, and demotes the rest. A key demoted by mistake causes an earlier miss, never wrong KV.

What the port takes and changes:
- v0.5.8rc1 has one L1 manager class per medium on BaseL1Manager (ShadowL1Manager included), behind the
  L1ManagerInterface protocol, and a StorageManager with several of them: demote_keys is BaseL1Manager's and the
  protocol's, and StorageManager.demote_l1_keys demotes in every L1 manager, as touch_cached_keys touches them.
- v0.5.8rc1's prefetch controller releases the read locks of the pages outside the window in _plan_load, row by row,
  where the load plan leaves them out, and demotes them there: _superseded_cells folds the hit over L1 and L2 as the
  plan does and returns the cells inside the hit before the window of their group ("full" fetching has no windows).
- end_session takes the keys chunk-major again, through the patch's _chunk_major_object_keys, which also returns
  each group's window and kv-rank count from its one read of the registration; v0.5.8rc1's _all_object_keys, whose
  order is unspecified, stays as it is for free_lookup_locks. The windows are the registration's, as
  resolve_prefetched_obj_keys reads them, since v0.5.8rc1's session no longer records them. Since #6125 a group
  stored once per tensor-parallel group has fewer kv-rank keys per chunk than a per-rank group, so
  usable_key_indices takes each group's kv-rank count instead of the world size. The usable keys are touched with
  touch_cached_keys, which also refreshes L2, and the superseded ones are found against a set built once.
- The test follows LMCache's lint (88 columns, the license header, import headings), opts out of the shared
  allocator, and also covers _superseded_cells, usable_key_indices and end_session with a group stored once, and a
  prefix prefetch through PrefetchController on a CPU DramL1Manager whose LRU must then hand out the superseded pages
  first.
